  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "0+I73vqLnRY6yUb0MLBd/SyGKgk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:20:21.159Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Concurrent reservations for the same `client_hash` are allowed deliberately, for three reasons:\n\n- **No uniqueness race.** Enforcing one live reservation per hash needs either a partial unique index \u2014 known broken under the `withSchema(\"attachments\")` migration setup, per the comment in migration 001 \u2014 or application-level locking, and it creates a rejected-reserver problem: what is the second client supposed to do while the first uploads?\n- **No reservation squatting.** If the second reserve were rejected in favour of an existing reservation, a client could reserve a hash it never intends to upload and **block that content for the 24-hour TTL**. Independent reservations make squatting harmless: anyone with the bytes can upload them.\n- **Commit-time convergence already exists.** Whichever upload finishes first inserts the row (`INSERT \u2026 ON CONFLICT (hash) DO NOTHING`); a later `send()` for the same hash lands in the dedup path and returns the existing record. Each `send()` soft-deletes only its own reservation, and stragglers age out via the TTL.\n\nThe cost is a bounded amount of duplicate upload bandwidth in the rare concurrent case \u2014 **strictly no worse than today**, where every duplicate upload transmits all bytes before dedup is detectable.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/attachments-hash-first.md` \u2014 Reservation semantics \u203a Concurrent reservations for the same hash\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:20:21.159Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:20:21.159Z"
    }
  },
  {
